  "answer_text": "This is a complex geopolitical question with a clear distinction between **legal status** and **effective control**. Here is a comprehensive overview:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfdb\ufe0f Legal / International Status: Crimea is Ukrainian territory\n\nLegally and in the eyes of most of the international community, Crimea is Ukrainian territory: the UN and leading Western institutions treat the peninsula as part of Ukraine and consider Russia's 2014 annexation invalid.\n\nIn 2014, the UN General Assembly adopted a non-binding resolution declaring the referendum invalid and reaffirming Ukraine's territorial integrity by a vote of 100 to 11, with 58 abstentions and 24 absent.\n\nThe lack of recognition from Ukraine and the international community is based primarily on the fact that the referendum included an option to join Russia while the region was under military occupation by Russia itself. The European Union, the United States, Canada, and several other nations condemned the decision to hold a referendum.\n\n---\n\n### \u2694\ufe0f How Did Russia Take Control?\n\nRussia's invasion of Ukraine started in Crimea in February 2014, when pro-Russian gunmen seized government buildings and the international airport, ultimately taking control of the peninsula. Those gunmen were often referred to as \"little green men\" because they weren't wearing any identifiable insignia \u2014 what became clear was that most of them were Russian soldiers. About a month later, Russian President Vladimir Putin illegally annexed the territory.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\uddfa\ufe0f Practical Reality: Russia Controls Crimea on the Ground\n\nPractically and politically, however, Crimea is administered by Russia and functions under Russian authority, creating a persistent gap between legal sovereignty and effective control that drives the ongoing conflict and diplomatic dispute.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd4a\ufe0f Current Diplomatic Situation (2025)\n\nFor more than a decade since the start of the invasion, Ukraine and its partners have never recognized Russia's annexation of Crimea. It remains unclear if either Russia or Ukraine will agree to any terms that could lead to a potential ceasefire, especially when it comes to the status of Crimea.\n\nFrom the Kremlin's perspective, Crimea's status isn't up for negotiation. Therefore, Russia would try to extract other US and Ukrainian concessions that go beyond Crimea.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcdd Summary\n\nIn short, **Russia controls Crimea in practice**, but **the vast majority of the world \u2014 including the United Nations \u2014 does not recognize Russian sovereignty over the peninsula**, considering it Ukrainian territory under illegal occupation. The question of Crimea's ultimate political future remains one of the most contentious issues in the ongoing Russia-Ukraine conflict.",
